# Untitled

## Low-Temperature Soft-Cover Deposition of Uniform Large-Scale Perovskite Films for High-Performance Solar Cells
Large-scale high-quality perovskite thin films are crucial to produce high-performance perovskite solar cells. However, for perovskite films fabricated by solvent-rich processes, film uniformity can be prevented by convection during thermal evaporation of the solvent. Here, a scalable low-temperature softcover deposition (LT-SCD) method is presented, where the thermal convection-induced defects in perovskite films are eliminated through a strategy of surface tension relaxation. Compact, homogeneous, and convection-induced defects-free perovskite films are obtained on an area of \(12\,\mathsf{cm}^{2}\), which enables a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of \(15.5\%\) on a solar cell with an area of \(5\,\mathsf{cm}^{2}\). This is the highest efficiency at this large cell area. A PCE of \(15.3\%\) is also obtained on a flexible perovskite solar cell deposited on the polyethylene terephthalate substrate owing to the advantage of presented low-temperature processing. Hence, the present LT-SCD technology provides a new non-spin-coating route to the deposition of large-area uniform perovskite films for both rigid and flexible perovskite devices.
Organic–inorganic halide hybrid perovskite materials have attracted tremendous attention in the field of thin-film photovoltaics because of their promising features such as solution processability, high crystallinity, direct and tunable band gap, and high hole/electron mobility.[1] The power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) of perovskite solar cells have improved rapidly from \(3.8\%\) to \(22.1\%\) in the past seven years.[1d] In view of future industrialization of perovskite solar cells, the current research has been expanded to the stability,[2] nontoxicity,[3] and film uniformity over large areas.[4] At present, the most widely
used low-cost method for the deposition of perovskite thin films is spin-coating process, in which most of the organic solvents are removed by centrifugal force, leading to high uniformity. Among tremendous efforts via spin-coating process, uniform perovskite films can be easily achieved by one-step coating or two-step sequential deposition. The spin-coating process is considered difficult to scale up because the film processed by spin coating is inhomogeneous over large areas. Other processes, such as doctor blading,[4b–d,f] slot-die coating,[4g,5] vapor-assisted deposition,[4a,6] and softcover deposition (SCD),[7] were recently proposed to fabricate large-area uniform perovskite films. In these processes, the excess solvents, such as dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO), γbutyrolactone, dimethylformamide (DMF), or 1-methyl-2-pyrrolidone
(NMP), have high boiling points and are mostly removed through thermal evaporation at relatively high temperature of over \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The thermal convection during solvent evaporation is inevitable because of the large temperature difference between the top and bottom of the solution, leading to the formation of regular microscopic defects like prominent boundaries or crevices between perovskite domains. These microscopic defects are detrimental to the formation of uniform perovskite films, which could inhibit the further development of large-area devices. Therefore, it is highly desired to develop low temperature non-spin-coating process for large-area uniform films. However, few studies have been reported on the elimination of convection-induced defects during thermal evaporation in scalable film formation process.
Here, we proposed a new method of low-temperature soft cover-assisted sequential deposition process (denoted as LT-SCD) to control the thermal convection in solvent-rich precursor solutions. Compact, homogeneous, and convection-induced defects-free perovskite films were obtained on an area of \(12\,\mathsf{cm}^{2}\). A solar cell \((5\,\mathsf{cm}^{2})\) with perovskite films deposited via the present LT-SCD method showed PCE of \(15.5\%\), which is the highest efficiency at this large cell area. The LT-SCD shows potential in application on flexible substrates because the process temperature is as low as \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). A solar cell on a polyethylene terephthalate (PET) film was fabricated and obtained a PCE of \(15.3\%\). Hence, the present LT-SCD technology provides a new non-spin-coating route to the deposition of large-area
uniform perovskite films for both rigid and flexible perovskite devices.
A schematic diagram of the LT-SCD method for sequentially deposited perovskite films is shown in Figure S1 in the Supporting Information. A substrate \((2 \times 6\ \mathrm{cm}^{2})\) was preheated on a hot plate at a temperature between 80 and \(140\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). A polyimide (PI) film, which has the properties of low contact angles with precursor solutions, smooth surface, and good solvent resistance,[7] was placed on the substrate to form a capillary. The capillary was helpful to spread a solution of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) in DMF, DMSO, or NMP into a uniform liquid film over the entire substrate. One end of the PI film was grasped by a robotic hand, which peeled off the PI film at a certain speed. During this process, the solvent in the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) solution evaporated, thereby depositing a \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) film on the substrate. A clean PI film was then placed on the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) film to form a capillary. The capillary was inserted into \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) (MAI)/isopropanol (IPA) solution, leading MAI to spread over the entire substrate by capillary to react with \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and form \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) perovskite. After completion of the reaction, the excess MAI was rinsed off with IPA and the sample was dried at about \(70\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The detailed process is described in the Supporting Information.
The surface morphology of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) films prepared by LT-SCD at a substrate temperature of \(80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) using DMF, DMSO, or NMP as solvents was observed by an optical microscope (Figure 1). The quasi-solid \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-DMSO and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-NMP films contained observable microscopic defects with typical cellular patterns that broke the film uniformity, which indicates the solvent property affecting the film uniformity largely. We also observed the surface topography of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-DMF, \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-DMSO, and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-NMP films deposited at higher temperature of 110 and \(140\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure S2, Supporting Information). Defects with cellular patterns were also observed in both \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-DMSO and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-NMP films. From the optical images, the size of the cellular patterns
increased with temperature from \(\approx5\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) at \(80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to \(\approx50\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) at \(140\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-DMSO and from \(\approx5\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) at \(80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to \(\approx20\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) at \(140\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-NMP. Such cellular patterns of defects exhibit temperature dependence, which indicates the film uniformity is largely influenced by the thermally induced convection.[4d,8] As illustrated in Figure 1D, cellular patterns can be formed within a liquid film when it is heated from the bottom slowly and uniformly, where the thermal convection of solvents is driven by the surface tension gradient resulting from the vertical temperature difference.[9]
One of the major factors for thermal convection is the surface tension difference \((\Delta\gamma)\) between the bottom and the top of the solution. The surface tension of DMF is much less than those of DMSO and NMP, and the values decrease monotonically with the increase of temperature (Figure S3, Supporting Information). Due to the temperature difference \((\Delta T)\) within the bottom-heated liquid film (Figure S4, Supporting Information), \(\Delta\gamma\) is established and drives the liquid convection from the point with low surface tension to a higher one with cellular patterns (Figure 1D). As shown in Figure 2A, \(\Delta\gamma\) of DMF decreased much faster than those of DMSO and NMP during evaporation, suggesting a rapid relaxation of driving force from surface tension. We further assessed the surface-tension-driven convection by the dimensionless Marangoni number \((M_{\mathrm{a}})\), which is expressed as[9,10]
\[
M_{\mathrm{a}}=-\frac{\mathrm{d}\gamma}{\mathrm{d}T}\frac{\Delta Td}{\rho vk}
\]
where \(\gamma\) is the surface tension, \(\rho\) is the density, \(\nu\) is the kinematic viscosity, \(k\) is the thermal diffusivity, \(\Delta T\) is the temperature difference between the bottom and surface of a liquid film, and \(d\) is the thickness of the liquid film. Since \(M_{\mathrm{a}}\) decreases
with the reduction of the liquid film thickness, we reduced the solution thickness as thin as possible by elevating peeling speed \((55\,\mathrm{mm\,\,s^{-1}})\) of PI film to reduce the convection.[7] We also found that \(M_{\mathrm{a}}\) decreased more rapidly for DMF than other solvents (Figure 2B), indicating the rapidly decreasing effect of surface tension difference on convection. The rapid decrease in \(M_{\mathrm{a}}\) allows the formation of a quasi-solid film before cellular convection occurred, which explains the absence of cellular patterns in \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) -DMF films. In contrast, the values of \(M_{\mathrm{a}}\) for DMSO and NMP decreased at slower rates, indicating that the strong surface tension can drive the convection during enough long time and form the typical cellular patterns.
One of the difficulties in forming uniform low-temperature solution processed films lies in the slow rate of removing liquid solvents, especially for non-spin-coating processes, where the surface tension leads to liquid convection or other inhomogeneous motions during long-time processing. Currently, scalable film fabrication processes usually require high-processing temperatures to enable rapid film crystallization (125, 190, and \(210\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for doctor blading,[4b] hot casting,[11] and SCD,[7] respectively). However, it is still hard to avoid cellular convection-induced defects in perovskite films.[4d] Besides, increasing the processing temperature is undesirable due to its energy consumption and limitation of application. LT-SCD provides a platform to control the thermal convection process, which can preserve heat and reduce the temperature gradient within the liquid film owing to the heat insulation by the covered PI film (Figure S5, Supporting Information).[7] The preserved heat can facilitate solvent evaporation after removing PI film (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Therefore, the heat preservation mechanism using a PI covering layer allows low-temperature operation to prevent formation of convection defects.
In sequential deposition processes, perovskite films inherit the morphology of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) films, we therefore further investigated the morphology of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) films prepared via LT-SCD at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with solvent of DMF (Figure S7, Supporting Information). The \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) film was very smooth within the first \(18\,\mathrm{s}\) after the removal of PI film. Acicular \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) began to appear from \(25\,\mathrm{s}\) , and the needlelike shape of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) became more prominent over time. The \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)
film also changed from a reflective light yellow film \((10\,\mathrm{s})\) to a rough dark yellow film \((70\,\mathrm{s})\) . We measured Fourier transform infrared spectra of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) films (Figure S8, Supporting Information). The peaks were assigned to \(\nu_{\mathrm{s}}\) \(\left(C\mathrm{H}_{3}\right)\) \((2930\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1})\) , \(\nu\left(C{=}0\right)\) \((1665\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1})\) , \(\nu\) \(\scriptstyle(C-N)\) \((1496\,\ c m^{-1})\) , \(b\) \(\scriptstyle(C-N-H)\) \((1389\,\ c m^{-1})\) , \(\nu_{\mathrm{as}}\) \(\scriptstyle(C-N)\) \((1256\ \ c m^{-1})\) , \(r\) \(\left(C\mathrm{H}_{3}\right)\) \((1098\ \ c m^{-1})\) , \(\nu_{\mathrm{s}}\) \(\scriptstyle(\mathrm{N-CH}_{3})\) \((865\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})\) , \(\nu\) \(\scriptstyle(0=C-N)\) \((661\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})\) . Strong vibrations of DMF appeared in the spectra obtained in the first \(18\,\mathrm{~s~}\) , suggesting DMF remained in the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) film at this point. The intensity of these peaks decayed markedly over time, and then changed little after \(70\,\mathrm{~s~}\) . The fresh \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) film contained residual DMF because DMF and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) can coordinate through the formation of a \(\mathrm{Pb-O}\) bond (bond length: 2.431 Å) to form \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) •DMF.[12] We believe that the amorphous \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) film obtained in the first \(18\,\mathrm{~s~}\) is more likely to provide a homogeneous perovskite thin film than a rougher one obtained after a longer period.
Figure S9A–C in the Supporting Information depicts scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) -DMF films prepared at 80, 110, and \(140\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , respectively (denoted as \(80{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) , \(110{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) , and \(140{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) , respectively). The images reveal that \(110{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and \(140{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) had poor coverage and homogeneity with island morphology, owing to the rapid crystallization of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) by evaporation of DMF at a temperature higher than \(110\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . In contrast, the low-temperature processed \(80{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) film possessed a smooth morphology without convection-related defects, which is prerequisite to obtain a perovskite film without microscopic defects because the perovskite is formed on the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) framework. Bearing this in mind, we prepared \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) films at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and then exposed them to MAI solution \((25\mathrm{~mg~mL^{-1}})\) via capillary immersion about \(10~\mathrm{s}\) after the completion of PI removal. As shown in Figure S10 in the Supporting Information, the optical and top-view SEM images of the perovskite films formed from \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) -DMSO and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) -NMP showed poor coverage with large convectional defects due to the poor morphology of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) films, while uniform perovskite film was obtained from \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) -DMF, suggesting the morphology inheritance of the final perovskite film from \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) . Figure 3A shows the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the perovskite films from the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) -DMF film with different dipping time in MAI solution. The peak at
about \(12.67^{\circ}\) characteristic of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) (001) was strong for the first 1 min and then disappeared after immersion for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\), suggesting a thorough and rapid transformation from amorphous \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) to perovskite. This rapid transformation occurred because residual DMF in the quasi-solid \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) film was easily replaced by MAI,[13] consistent with previous reports.[14] The separation between the (004) and (220) peaks indicates that the obtained perovskite was tetragonal (space group \(I4/mcm\), \(a=8.8743\ \mathrm{\AA}\), \(c=12.6708\ \mathrm{\AA}\)) and highly crystallized.[15] The evolution of the XRD patterns also reveals that the conversion of amorphous \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) to perovskite caused the (220) peak to strengthen.
UV–vis spectra of the perovskite films obtained by immersion in MAI/IPA for 1, 5, and \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) are presented in Figure 3B. The steep cutoff at about \(788\ \mathrm{nm}\) is consistent with a \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) band gap of about \(1.57\ \mathrm{eV}\). To examine the variations in light absorbance with immersion time, we used the UV–vis absorbance at \(750\ \mathrm{nm}\) as an index, which is proportional to the film thickness according to the Beer–Lambert law. Specifically, the absorbances at \(750\ \mathrm{nm}\) were 0.81, 1.16, and 1.31 for films immersed for 1, 5, and \(10\ \mathrm{min}\), respectively. Thus, the immersion for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) provides a perovskite film with high absorbance, which is desirable for light trapping in perovskite devices. Top-view SEM images of the perovskite films obtained after immersing in MAI/IPA for 1, 5, and \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) are shown in Figure 3C. The film was not dense after immersion for \(1\ \mathrm{min}\), which was mainly caused by the dissolution of DMF in IPA. The perovskite films formed after immersion for 5 and \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) were much denser. The perovskite obtained by immersion for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) was flat and compact, with grain sizes in the range of \(200{-}400\ \mathrm{nm}\). An atomic force microscopy image of the film obtained after immersion for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) is illustrated in Figure S11 in the Supporting Information. The root mean square surface
roughness for this film is about \(16.9\ \mathrm{nm}\), indicating a very smooth surface. The formation of high-quality perovskite thin films is attributed to the amorphous quasi-solid \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) precursor film containing residual DMF. It has been reported that Ostwald ripening occurs during immersion of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) in a high concentration of MAI in IPA for a long time, resulting in grain coarsening.[16] We extended the capillary immersion time to \(25\ \mathrm{min}\), which also led to grain coarsening (Figure S12, Supporting Information). We believe that even though the reaction consumed a certain amount of MAI, MAI was continuously transported to the substrate surface by capillary action, which kept the MAI concentration high for a long time, resulting in the observed Ostwald ripening.
The perovskite film thickness in LT-SCD can be tuned by changing the peeling speed and concentration of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}/\mathrm{DMF}\) solution. Perovskite film thicknesses are plotted as a function of peeling speed in Figure S13 in the Supporting Information. The thicknesses of the perovskite films were controllable between \(250\ \mathrm{nm}\) and about \(2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) for the peeling speed from 150 to \(5\ \mathrm{mm}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\). Figure S14 in the Supporting Information shows the uniform \(12\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\) area perovskite film fabricate with LT-SCD. A planar solar cell (area: \(5\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\)) with a structure of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\):F (FTO)/NiO/perovskite/phenyl-\(\mathrm{C}_{61}\)-butyric acid methyl ester (PCBM)/bathocuproine (BCP)/Ag was made to evaluate the optoelectronic properties of large-scale perovskite films prepared by LT-SCD. As shown in Figure 4, We achieved a PCE of \(15.5\%\), open-circuit voltage (\(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{OC}}\)) of \(1.03\ \mathrm{V}\), short-circuit current density (\(\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}\)) of \(21.5\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), and fill factor (FF) of \(70.1\%\) under simulated AM 1.5G solar light (\(100\ \mathrm{mW}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\)) with an illuminated aperture area of \(5\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\) defined by an aperture on a black mask, which is the highest efficiency at this large cell size. The incident photon-to-current conversion efficiency
(IPCE) of the device is shown in Figure S15 in the Supporting Information, and an integrated \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) of \( 21.4\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2} \) is well corresponding to the \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) from the \( J{-}V \) curves. We also fabricated small-area devices \((0.09\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{2})\) with the same structure, and the \( J{-}V \) curves of the champion device in forward and reverse scanning are shown in Figure S16 in the Supporting Information. In the forward scanning from 0 to \( 1.2\;\mathrm{V} \), it yielded a \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) of \( 21.5\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2} \), a \( V_{\mathrm{OC}} \) of \( 1.07\;\mathrm{V} \), an FF of \( 0.77\% \), and a PCE of \( 17.7\% \), and showed small hysteresis. This promising device performance attests to the high quality of the perovskite film formed by LT-SCD. To show the uniformity of the perovskite films via LT-SCD, we fabricated eight small devices on different parts of a large-area substrate and reproduced four batches. The PCE distribution of 32 cells from different subareas is shown in Figure S17 in the Supporting Information. The narrow distribution in PCEs indicates a high uniformity and reproducibility of the large-area perovskite films. Since LT-SCD markedly lowers the film preparation temperature, it is possible to fabricate perovskite solar cells on flexible substrates. We fabricated solar cells with the same invert structure on flexible PET films by LT-SCD (Figure S18, Supporting Information). Perovskite film fabrication was carried out as described above on an FTO substrate. The flexible device with an area of \( 0.09\;\mathrm{cm}^{2} \) displayed a PCE of \( 15.3\% \), \( V_{\mathrm{OC}} \) of \( 1.09\;\mathrm{V} \), \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) of \( 19.5\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2} \), and FF of \( 72.3\% \), as shown in the \( J{-}V \) curve in Figure S18C in the Supporting Information. The IPCE of the device gave an integrated \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) of \( 19.1\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2} \), consistent with that from the \( J{-}V \) curve. These results indicate that high-quality perovskite films can also be fabricated on flexible substrates by LT-SCD.
We developed a scalable low-temperature sequential deposition method to fabricate perovskite films in ambient air. By studying the effects of solvent convection on defects formation, convection-induced defects were effectively eliminated by LT-SCD. Furthermore, perovskite formation by LT-SCD was rapid and complete because of the molecular exchange between DMF and MAI in the precursor \( \mathrm{PbI}_{2} \) film. Smooth and compact perovskite films with an area of \( 12\;\mathrm{cm}^{2} \) were obtained by LT-SCD on FTO substrate, and a maximum PCE of \( 15.5\% \) was achieved on a solar cell with an aperture area of \( 5\;\mathrm{cm}^{2} \), which
is the highest efficiency at this large cell size. A flexible solar cell was also fabricated by LT-SCD since the process temperature was \( 80\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), and a PCE of \( 15.3\% \) was obtained. The results illustrate LT-SCD is a convenient and versatile new route for the sequential deposition of large-area perovskite films both on rigid and flexible substrates, demonstrating great potential for future commercialization.
